---
// Long-form homepage guide. Written to be genuinely readable first and
// keyword-complete second — the target terms all appear because the topic
// demands them, not because they were sprinkled in.
import Container from '../layout/Container.astro';
---

<Container class="py-12 sm:py-16" as="section" id="guide">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="caption-mono uppercase text-mute">The complete guide</p>
    <h2 class="display-lg mt-3 text-ink">Webcam test: everything the tool checks, and why it matters</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        A free webcam test is the quickest way to answer a question that costs people meetings
        every day: does my camera actually work, and do I look the way I think I do? Running a free
        webcam test here takes about a minute, needs no download and no account, and gives you a
        real answer before an interview, a lesson, a telehealth appointment or a stream — rather
        than five awkward minutes at the start of a call while everyone waits.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">What an online webcam test actually does</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        When you press start, your browser asks for permission and then hands the camera feed
        straight to this page. The preview you see is the live stream from your device. Every
        number reported next to it — resolution, frame rate, aspect ratio, bitrate, brightness,
        contrast, saturation and colour count — is calculated in your browser from frames that
        never leave your computer. That is the important difference between a webcam test online
        and an app you install: there is no upload, no server-side processing, and nothing to
        uninstall afterwards.
      </p>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Most people only need the first ten seconds of this. If your face appears, the camera
        works. The rest of the test exists for the harder question — whether the picture is good
        enough that nobody on the call is quietly distracted by it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">How to run the webcam test</h3>
      <ol class="body-md space-y-3 text-body">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">1. Press “Start test”.</strong> Nothing installs. The button
          triggers the same browser API that Zoom, Teams and Google Meet use in the browser.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">2. Allow camera access.</strong> Your browser will ask, every
          time, for every site — that prompt is a protection, not a warning about this page. If no
          prompt appears, you have answered before and the browser remembered.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">3. Pick the right device.</strong> Laptops with a docking
          station, a capture card, or software like OBS often expose several cameras. Use the
          dropdown to step through them until you see yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">4. Read the results.</strong> Health checks flag anything that
          would look poor on a call and tell you what to change, rather than leaving you with a
          number and no context.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">Resolution, frame rate and bitrate</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Three numbers decide how sharp and how smooth you look. <strong class="text-ink"
          >Resolution</strong
        > is detail: 720p is the practical minimum for a call that looks crisp, 1080p is excellent,
        and anything under 480p looks soft on a large monitor. We report both the resolution you are
        getting right now and the maximum your camera supports, because browsers and conferencing
        apps routinely negotiate a lower one to save bandwidth.
      </p>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        <strong class="text-ink">Frame rate</strong> is smoothness. 30 fps is the standard;
        below 15 fps your movements visibly stutter. We count the frames your browser actually
        presents rather than repeating the figure printed on the box, so the reading reflects your
        real conditions — including the lighting in the room you are sitting in.
        <strong class="text-ink">Bitrate</strong> is measured by encoding a short sample of your
        own stream, which is the only honest way to produce that number in a browser.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">A combined mic and webcam test</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Video is only half of a call. A mic and webcam test in one place saves you checking two
        sites, so the microphone level meter starts alongside the camera: speak normally and the
        bar should move well into the middle of its range. If it barely twitches, your input gain
        is too low or the wrong device is selected. If it sits pinned at the top, the signal is
        clipping and you will sound harsh to everyone else.
      </p>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Speakers are the half that people forget entirely, which is why a call sometimes starts
        with someone saying they cannot hear anything. The speaker panel plays a short tone through
        the left channel, the right channel, or both, so you can confirm your output and your
        headphones before you join. There is a dedicated
        <a class="text-link hover:text-link-deep" href="/mic-test">microphone test</a> if audio is
        the only thing you need to check.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">Webcam test Windows 10 and Windows 11 users can run in any browser</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        A webcam test on Windows 10 works exactly as it does anywhere else — the browser does the
        work, so there is no driver or app to install — but Windows keeps its own camera permission list underneath the browser, and that is
        where most Windows-specific failures come from. Open
        <span class="code-font">Settings → Privacy &amp; security → Camera</span> and confirm three
        switches: “Camera access” is on, “Let apps access your camera” is on, and — the one almost
        everyone misses — “Let desktop apps access your camera” is on as well. Browsers count as
        desktop apps, so a camera that works in the Windows Camera app can still fail in Chrome
        while that third switch is off. Windows 11 uses the same path with slightly different
        wording.
      </p>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        On macOS the equivalent lives in
        <span class="code-font">System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Camera</span>, and macOS
        requires you to quit the browser completely before the change takes effect. Chromebooks,
        Android phones and iPhones all work without any of this, provided the page is on HTTPS.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">Which browsers and devices work</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Every current browser supports camera access: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, Opera and
        Brave, across Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS, Android and iOS. The one hard requirement is
        a secure connection — browsers refuse camera access to any page served over plain
        <span class="code-font">http://</span>, which is why a local development site never shows a
        preview no matter which permission you grant.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">What makes this webcam test site different</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Most tools stop at “your camera works”. This one shows you the two things a large mirrored
        preview actively hides. The call preview renders your feed at the sizes a real meeting uses
        — speaker view, gallery tile and thumbnail — and un-mirrored, which is how everyone except
        you sees your face. The recording panel captures a few seconds and plays it back, because
        watching yourself move is the only reliable way to judge lighting and framing.
      </p>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Where your camera and browser allow it, the camera controls go further still and adjust the
        hardware itself: zoom, brightness, exposure and focus, changed live so you can fix a dim
        picture instead of only being told it is dim. Rule-of-thirds framing guides help you place
        your eyes on the upper line, and a one-click diagnostics report collects your browser, OS
        and every measurement into text you can paste into a support ticket.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">If the camera does not appear</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        Four causes explain nearly every failure: a blocked permission, another application holding
        the camera, the wrong device selected, or a privacy shutter over the lens. Close Zoom,
        Teams, Meet and OBS completely — including windows minimised to the system tray — then
        reload. Our <a class="text-link hover:text-link-deep" href="/troubleshooting">troubleshooting guide</a>
        covers each one with the exact settings path for every major browser and operating system,
        and decodes the specific error your browser reports.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display-sm mt-10 text-ink">Privacy</h3>
      <p class="body-md text-body">
        A free webcam test should not cost you your privacy. This site is a set of static files
        with no endpoint that could receive video, so your feed is never uploaded, and snapshots
        and recordings are created on your device and stay there unless you choose to download
        them. Pressing stop releases the camera immediately and your indicator light goes out —
        your own independent confirmation. The
        <a class="text-link hover:text-link-deep" href="/privacy">privacy page</a> sets out the
        detail in plain language.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Container>
